/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423691} {:answer/id #uuid "a1250c4b-23e3-59f3-a490-f89d27cddab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423767} {:answer/id #uuid "a39a1597-fd98-5261-9c1a-cffbe2cd582d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423769} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e0e18f-beed-55ab-95b9-7b8cf6b2e8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b58e38-8d8a-545b-a564-39cbf09af580" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423851}] :question/id #uuid "bd607d3f-88f1-5057-a261-73a39b9023d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-363834" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457a3842-15d8-5552-b245-a07a1675cac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430278} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd9661f-8ecd-52f0-bb59-99f0d617f957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430285} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf668ec-1209-510a-bec2-b439f7a0fc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430285} :answer-field/id #uuid "111bbd61-ed5a-5717-8d1a-2107732df5dc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b020d76e-e1ef-5bcd-91cd-80bb99bd3362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430312} {:answer/id #uuid "b66681db-9ca9-5aa2-be1a-2b2722e717f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430318} {:answer/id #uuid "e79293f1-0fa4-5fd9-a0c9-d75bdda1898a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430332}] :answer-field/correct 