could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10aae14c-c7e9-55c2-94a5-1341139b9ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423685} {:answer/id #uuid "737c7d77-d190-5ada-b444-b38ee3ace3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423742} {:answer/id #uuid "c57dbd27-146c-531b-b2b5-f9e7ddef0e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423788} {:answer/id #uuid "d2402fbc-26fe-5ab7-83ad-5e5b049eb0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423797} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e20c49-8ea9-5471-b841-13939981e51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423788} :answer-field/id #uuid "d85afa76-6386-5290-9709-46b25b7de673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423850}] :question/id #uuid "7cbede22-0844-5470-8655-1901a08d01cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140963" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "511e043d-9e27-54d0-847f-5a97039f88c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answe