}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223c8e67-5171-5ea9-813f-ad2c34f62661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496728} {:answer/id #uuid "78c5338f-e189-5564-b2bd-1367f0bf0775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496912} {:answer/id #uuid "b22038de-df21-574b-a2d3-9931b67c69bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497022} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e9bd21-b777-5f10-97d3-56334fae5657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497115} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b4cead-3dc7-5c6a-98ef-f3fe49303b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80968a1-b5d3-5a74-a9ab-07fd72693189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497315}] :question/id #uuid "2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140962" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161839 :question/id #uuid "1b9d8b05-cdb6-555b-b68c-b46000bb6650" :question/math-academy-id "e-8798" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c23820c65f2ff70de6eb9f10e6e99f6daaf8096e0cb873fb2c3d68946e2f4c1.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/a1/a1256c5e8c454d905c1550c06bf3aa413cc17acaeba0e6e2cbbda68cfc05ac50.svg)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} x_2- \\dfrac{1}{2} x_3 = 0   \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\sqrt2x_1 + x_2-x_3 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/a6/a619cda4912587e8c4dc4da0bc7d0a575d1c10f1adba2137099f347f6d7eabb3.svg)\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n![](images/22/22311430e9529d48b9d0460011e6baceacfbfcc54902439e917bfdc3dd04d5db.svg)\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing the orthogonal vectors $\\mathbf{c}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U{:}$   \n\n![](images/66/661e2d509c842bac2a5427e82c3973feb972b540dbf405be7a023a7802cf93fb.svg)\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1a27507e18b6bcb0353668919f7fb5c356afa1f07ab77f73422fe3236e78fe8a.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #