answer/id #uuid "7c7a5ad0-60ed-5522-a987-5398faa11e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370845} :answer-field/id #uuid "f874152d-9e9f-5f7e-82d8-7c8e8905137a" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370841}] :question/id #uuid "d647ef3a-6586-59df-8413-264bc5ba09f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-363844" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-10}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-11}} |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-10}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-11}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb23bb5-3483-5614-b88a-de724eaace0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423688} {:answer/id #uuid "632a3226-0edc-509f-99a3-4d9c96f743ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423729} {:answer/id #uuid "7218d9af-95a8-55cf-800f-61814c401415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423741} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9e14f5-017e-56b0-8dc0-4c4f65c61b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423729} :answer-field/id #uuid "14410804-799e-56d0-9d89-7e2196853f56" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58116699-dc0e-5875-8617-121d3e8874d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423719} {:answer/id #uuid "9c42c3c4-2aba-5894-96bd-2074694edbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423763} {:answer/id #uuid "c267c1c4-add4-5231-a874-6fcfa0f47fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423719} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e151b3c-d415-573c-81b7-688b8b302f25" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.t