wer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6548d6b5-487d-54bf-ae20-579bcc0c1f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430288} {:answer/id #uuid "95c3ac78-d00e-59a3-84fc-7c652182e808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430303} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c5b684-52c7-5af3-b745-dc21f72e1303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430288} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc6db4d2-ea1d-5421-a0db-e24e2e0720aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430358}] :question/id #uuid "0ba1751d-87a5-51c1-9c47-8e0f1eb46649" :question/math-academy-id "q-363846" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21cd4f8b-9498-5d21-baab-074e7d02e4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525282} {:answer/id #uuid "83d6bc2e-d7c0-5db9-9f3d-bd4cdf4f6f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525538} {:answer/id #uuid "8940d80e-582b-5d7c-8642-73f87b9c0924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525551} {:answer/id #uuid "bccc676a-883d-57ad-9f71-0e94856d6b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525551} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd819e0-daee-5f8c-b3ed-dda09a79f3bb" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce43be3-6287-5a43-8e7e-f0f6dbb70a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525358} {:answer/id #uuid "403732de-7db9-5b49-9aca-47b26030eeee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525365} {:answer/id #uuid "d04f4968-c94c-5bbd-b17a-854dad443dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525724} {:answer/id #uuid "d396567f-f526-5dec-9323-98393255b386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525358} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0218e7-a200-5318-bd2c-22f226c4e6de" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c9cece-cd9f-501b-a7a8-4f7ab7cc66d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\s