e {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548543} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2e3317-5784-5bcd-a80a-10ea98305234" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2e59d6-51a7-5fe2-89d0-05e8293c8d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548499} {:answer/id #uuid "52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548510} {:answer/id #uuid "71ccef85-acdf-5822-b7cd-1c90700e66a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548566} {:answer/id #uuid "cecfcc4e-2ba5-5f52-b0ef-793f85fe11dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548510} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a85cef-fa7c-5a3f-95d7-68572b164e28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548972}] :question/id #uuid "84533a55-9193-5a76-a277-23539897c843" :question/math-academy-id "q-363663" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-4)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3133 :topic/title "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808} :activity/id #uuid "37dbb8fe-57e2-5578-a0d2-de00af96f5ed" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & ={{(\\,7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}-{{(\\,1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{13}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix}$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" :tutorial/id #uuid "99828b7b-b501-5509-8853-d17316824f5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57f8ef60-6efd-5887-929c-a96714976291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07"} :step/id #uuid "8e5e1cdf-9076-58fb-b468-401ba770db35" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061007 :tutorial/content "If the determinant of a square matrix $A$ is equal to zero, then we say that $A$ is a **singular** matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is singular, then it does not have a multiplicative inverse." :tutorial/id #uuid "2dc6054b-777f-5106-9e65-71bede9f6b49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9176 :tutorial/title "Singular Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "e038964f-9424-5512-b66c-59d652b56e87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93"} :step/id #uuid "2bf05412-402c-54cb-80bb-3ec55c979a73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061008 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthat has an unknown constant $k$ as one of the entries, and we are given that $A$ is singular. How do we find all the possible values of $k?$\n\nOne way is to use the formula for the determinant of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k,$ and then make an equation with the given determinant to solve for $k.$\n\nSo first, let's use the formula for the determinant on our matrix to get an expression in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1-2)-2(k-0)+3(2k-0) \\\\  & =-3-2k+6k \\\\  & =4k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we are told that $\\det(A) =0,$ let's equate the above expression to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ 4k-3 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $k$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "55643210-75d5-50e5-a63b-30b951b8a201" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9177 :tutorial/title "Solving for a Variable"} :step/id #uuid "1db34187-4316-57e4-9ac9-a00c7f32f091" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb"} :step/id #uuid "661a91c2-23f6-5cc7-bb2a-4955ef3942cb"}] :activity/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059886}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059452 :question/id #uuid "14e05d4f-373c-5d1f-aa59-7ee52466480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14762" :question/problem "Find the value of ![](images/8e/8e7935fce161e2a1ed706ec03bb5cd202e8c4650ba01ca5e4633f9f1c121d04a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0(8+96)+3(8+48)+6(-96+48) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 56+6\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =168-288 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534253 :question-generator/id #uuid "903c2ba0-542c-56fb-a041-ff23bc8f9cdb" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186059847.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98f5e0e0-c541-5d5e-b120-030d10523d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058291} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a5c589-e2fa-52d4-bdc2-98052caa1a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058292} {:answer/id #uuid "050bb88d-1c57-5dcc-9cba-c9dddc9dd491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058293} {:answer/id #uuid "287927b3-cd33-5f0b-a23f-da8c2dcc0f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058294} {:answer/id #uuid "5a082a8b-804e-590d-a86c-ce9acb67344c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058295}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ee70b-e1cf-592b-a7b7-c7aab8026e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058290}] :question/id #uuid "30744632-a42a-5c58-b02f-ea2283f92b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38128" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}$."} {:db/id #ed